### Demonstrating RegExp multiline flag behavior in JavaScript

Source: https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/global_objects/regexp/multiline/index.md

This JavaScript example illustrates the `multiline` accessor property and the functional impact of the `m` flag on regular expressions. It compares two regex patterns, one with and one without the `m` flag, showing how the `^` anchor's matching behavior changes from the start of the entire string to the start of each line when the `m` flag is present. The output verifies the `multiline` property's value and the `test` method's results for both cases.

```javascript
const regex1 = /^football/;
const regex2 = /^football/m;

console.log(regex1.multiline);
// Expected output: false

console.log(regex2.multiline);
// Expected output: true

console.log(regex1.test("rugby\nfootball"));
// Expected output: false

console.log(regex2.test("rugby\nfootball"));
// Expected output: true
```

--------------------------------

### Count Vowels in String using JavaScript Regular Expressions

Source: https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/guide/regular_expressions/character_classes/index.md

This snippet demonstrates how to count the occurrences of vowels (A, E, I, O, U, Y) in a given string using JavaScript regular expressions. It utilizes the 'g' flag for global matching and the 'i' flag for case-insensitive matching. The 'match()' method returns an array of all matches, and its 'length' property provides the total count.

```js
const aliceExcerpt =
  "There was a long silence after this, and Alice could only hear whispers now and then.";
const regexpVowels = /[aeiouy]/gi;

console.log("Number of vowels:", aliceExcerpt.match(regexpVowels).length);
// Number of vowels: 26
```

--------------------------------

### Global and case-insensitive replacement in JavaScript

Source: https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/global_objects/string/replace/index.md

Use a regular expression with 'g' and 'i' flags to replace all occurrences of a pattern regardless of case.

```javascript
const re = /apples/gi;
const str = "Apples are round, and apples are juicy.";
const newStr = str.replace(re, "oranges");
console.log(newStr); // oranges are round, and oranges are juicy.
```

--------------------------------

### Non-global regex throws error with replaceAll()

Source: https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/global_objects/string/replaceall/index.md

When using a regular expression as the search pattern, it must have the global flag (g). A non-global regex will throw a TypeError.

```JavaScript
"aabbcc".replaceAll(/b/, ".");
// TypeError: replaceAll must be called with a global RegExp
```

```JavaScript
"aabbcc".replaceAll(/b/g, ".");
("aa..cc");
```

### Regular expressions > Regex flags

Source: https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/regular_expressions/index.md

Flags are special parameters that can change the way a regular expression is interpreted or the way it interacts with the input text. Each flag corresponds to one accessor property on the `RegExp` object. Common flags include `g` for global search, `i` for case-insensitive search, `m` to make `^` and `$` match the start and end of each line instead of the entire string, `s` to allow `.` to match newline characters, `u` to treat a pattern as a sequence of Unicode code points, and `y` to perform a "sticky" search that matches starting at the current position. The `i`, `m`, and `s` flags can be enabled or disabled for specific parts of a regex using modifier syntax.

